1. CSS syntax and selectors
Every CSS rule usually has a selector and a declaration block.
    .card {
    background-color: white;
    padding: 20px;
      border-radius: 12px;
    }

- .card  — selector that targets elements with class card.
- background-color  — property being changed.
- white  — value assigned to that property.
- padding: 20px — space inside the element.
- border-radius: 12px — rounded corners.

Selector reference

Element	p {}	All paragraph elements
Class	.card {}	Elements with class="card"
ID	#header {}	The element with id="header"
Universal	* {}	All elements
Group	h1, p {}	Both headings and paragraphs
Descendant	.card p {}	Paragraphs anywhere inside a card
Child	.card > p {}	Direct child paragraphs
Multiple classes	.btn.primary {}	Elements with both classes
Attribute	input[type="email"] {}	Email inputs
Pseudo-class	.btn:hover {}	Button while hovered
Pseudo-element	p::first-letter {}	First letter of a paragraph

In React, use className instead of HTML's class attribute.
    function ProfileCard() {
        return (
        <div className="card">
          <h2>My Profile</h2>
          <p>Java Backend Developer</p>
          <button className="btn">View Profile</button>
        </div>
      );
    }